### Have independent healthcare policy analysts or organizations evaluated John Cornyn's plan and confirmed it provides the described guarantees for preexisting conditions?
Answer: No, independent analysis has not confirmed such guarantees; rather, a Commonwealth Fund analysis of the related "Ensuring Coverage for Patients with Pre-Existing Conditions Act" found that while it would maintain the ACA's rule prohibiting denial of coverage, it would "permit insurers to sell plans that entirely exclude coverage for treatment of an enrolled individual’s preexisting condition," leading to substantial increases in out-of-pocket costs.

Source URL: https://www.commonwealthfund.org/publications/issue-briefs/2018/nov/excluding-preexisting-conditions-impact-on-out-of-pocket-costs

## Final Judgement
Based on the recorded evidence, the key findings are that John Cornyn publicly endorsed the House Republican "Better Care Reconciliation Act" to repeal and replace the ACA. While that legislation included a provision barring insurers from denying coverage based on pre-existing conditions, it allowed states to waive essential health benefits requirements, effectively enabling insurers to offer "skinny plans" that could exclude coverage for treatments related to pre-existing conditions. This would increase out-of-pocket costs for those with serious health conditions. Furthermore, an independent analysis confirmed that similar legislative proposals would permit insurers to sell plans that entirely exclude coverage for a pre-existing condition's treatment. Crucially, the guarantee of issuing insurance policies to all applicants already existed under the Affordable Care Act, which Cornyn's supported legislation aimed to repeal and weaken.

The evidence is sufficient for a conclusion. The fact-check directly contradicts the implication of the claim. The claim asserts that John Cornyn's plan would guarantee that individuals with pre-existing conditions "will be covered." However, the evidence shows that the legislation he championed would have weakened the existing protections under the ACA, creating mechanisms that could allow insurers to deny meaningful coverage for those conditions while still satisfying the technical requirement to issue a plan.

Therefore, the claim is `refuted`. The recorded evidence explicitly shows that John Cornyn supported a plan that, while technically prohibiting the denial of insurance sales, would have undermined the comprehensive coverage guarantees for pre-existing conditions that were a cornerstone of the Affordable Care Act.

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
John Cornyn's campaign ad claimed his plan would guarantee coverage for people with preexisting conditions, but the legislation he publicly supported—the "Better Care Reconciliation Act"—while technically barring insurers from denying coverage based on preexisting conditions, allowed states to waive essential health benefits requirements, enabling "skinny plans" that could [exclude treatments for those conditions](https://www.theatlantic.com/business/archive/2017/06/ahca-senate-bill-preexisting-conditions/531375/). An [independent Commonwealth Fund analysis](https://www.commonwealthfund.org/publications/issue-briefs/2018/nov/excluding-preexisting-conditions-impact-on-out-of-pocket-costs) confirmed that similar proposals would permit insurers to sell plans entirely excluding coverage for a preexisting condition's treatment, substantially increasing out-of-pocket costs. Moreover, the basic guarantee of issuing policies to all applicants already existed under the [Affordable Care Act](https://abcnews.go.com/Politics/republicans-trumpet-pre-existing-condition-protections-votes-repeal/story?id=58597074), which Cornyn championed [repealing](https://www.cbsnews.com/philadelphia/news/cornyn-conservatives-wishful-thinking/), meaning the plan he supported would have weakened, not strengthened, existing preexisting condition protections.